        {
            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "Thanks for the suggestion!\n\nThe reason all buffers are force removed before reading a session is because the latter kind of assumes that it loads in a \"fresh\" Neovim instance. Otherwise it might become a mix of several sessions which I don't think is good.\n\nOn top of that, terminal buffers are saved and restored in sessions. Which would become problematic for this particular use case as current session (if available) is first written before reading the new one. And I personally think that built-in terminals should be fairly short lived and tied to the session.\n\nSo I am afraid this is better to be marked as not planned. It should be possible to replicated the requested behavior fairly easily by using `vim.cmd('source path/to/session')` instead of `MiniSessions.read()` while doing any existing buffer manipulation beforehand as you see fit.",
