        {
            "author_association": "NONE",
            "body": "Thanks for the reply!\n\nI know you have a lot of work to do as a maintainer of this large suite of plugins and neovim core so I don't want to bother you too much. I just want to add some context to my original post regardless of if this issue is not planned for you or anyone who might see this issue. \n\nThe use case is where you use sessions not only as a mechanism to restore your layout/state after a restart but also as a mechanism to switch between active projects on the same neovim instance. There are more use cases here where you might have just one or two buffers (maybe based on another plugin) which you would not want to kill when switching sessions. For example, you might use `overseer.nvim` (or any other task runner) that may output to a buffer which would be completely lost when you load a new session. This would apply for any plugin where it uses a buffer that is not backed by a file.\n\nI have terminals enabled in my `sessionoptions`. All I do is unlist any terminals in the session before load, delete everything else, then after the session loads, I replace the terminal buffer(s) that were created by the session with the existing one(s). Namely, each terminal you create is pinned to the session that created it and you don't see the terminals from other sessions (since they are still unlisted). This way, I can be working on a project repo, go deal with an issue in another project and then come back to to the same project with my terminal still there with anything I had running instead a fresh terminal created by the session. I actually wrote a local session plugin to do this here https://github.com/simifalaye/dotfiles/blob/a3609b1a43ee24a3527e98b6f3d3851411ca75a9/modules/neovim/dots/.config/nvim/plugin/01-session.lua#L124-L178\n\nAs you said, I could just source the session instead of calling `MiniSessions.read()`, BUT, I'd have to re-implement everything the `read` function does in order to still be compatible with the plugin such as calls to `H.detect_sessions()` (since this is an internal function). More importantly, if `mini.sessions` changes anything later on to it's internal state management inside the call to `read`, I'd silently break the plugin for myself when I update since it wouldn't match what the new `read` function is doing internally.\n\nI use most of the mini.nvim suite of plugins so I thought it made more sense just to ask (or even implement myself) a few lines of code to support this option rather than using my custom plugin just to make this one line configurable. Even an option to just disable the call to `bwipeout` and then handle the cleanup of the layout in the `pre_read` hook would do it.",
