        {
            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "> The use case is where you use sessions not only as a mechanism to restore your layout/state after a restart but also as a mechanism to switch between active projects on the same neovim instance.\n\nI do view sessions and the mechanism to switch between active projects. That's how I've been using them all the time. And the decision in this issue is done to support this usage.\n\n> For example, you might use `overseer.nvim` (or any other task runner) that may output to a buffer which would be completely lost when you load a new session. This would apply for any plugin where it uses a buffer that is not backed by a file.\n\nAnd the behavior of \"completely lost after loading new session\" is the one that I (currently) think is the most reasonable. Mostly because these temporary buffers are tightly coupled with the projects. The task runner is a very good example of this: tasks differ from project to project and so should buffers that work with them.",
